<span id="S5.T1.8.1.1.1.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">12</span>
<span id="S5.T1.8.1.1.1.6.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">[2, 4, 6, 8]</span>
<span id="S5.T1.8.1.1.1.6.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">[<a href="#A7.F32" title="Figure 32 ‣ G.1.1 Dense Mixture-of-Experts ‣ G.1 Verification of Linear Mode Connectivity across diverse configurations ‣ Appendix G Experimental Results ‣ On Linear Mode Connectivity of Mixture-of-Experts Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>, <a href="#A7.F33" title="Figure 33 ‣ G.1.1 Dense Mixture-of-Experts ‣ G.1 Verification of Linear Mode Connectivity across diverse configurations ‣ Appendix G Experimental Results ‣ On Linear Mode Connectivity of Mixture-of-Experts Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>, <a href="#A7.F34" title="Figure 34 ‣ G.1.1 Dense Mixture-of-Experts ‣ G.1 Verification of Linear Mode Connectivity across diverse configurations ‣ Appendix G Experimental Results ‣ On Linear Mode Connectivity of Mixture-of-Experts Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>, <a href="#A7.F35" title="Figure 35 ‣ G.1.1 Dense Mixture-of-Experts ‣ G.1 Verification of Linear Mode Connectivity across diverse configurations ‣ Appendix G Experimental Results ‣ On Linear Mode Connectivity of Mixture-of-Experts Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>]</span></span>
</span></span></p>
</span></div>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.11348v1/LMC_main.svg" id="S5.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/87;" width="476" height="87"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>LMC curves for ViT (subplots 1-3) and GPT-2 (subplot 4) with a 4-expert MoE replacement at the <span id="S5.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">first</span> Transformer layer, on CIFAR-100, ImageNet21k<math id="S5.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>CIFAR-100, ImageNet-1k, and One Billion Word datasets, respectively. Plots show consistent low-loss linear interpolation paths between fine-tuned models, indicating strong linear mode connectivity.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Matching Order of Experts.</span> In MoEs, experts and their gating can be reordered without affecting functionality. We propose two methods to align the expert order between <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, using LAP.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Method 1 - Gating Weights.</span> To address the translation-invariance of the softmax function in the gating, we center the weights and biases of the gating. For <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, these are <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{W}_{i}=W_{i}-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{m=1}^{n}W_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{W}_{i}=W_{i}-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{m=1}^{n}W_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{b}_{i}=b_{i}-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{m=1}^{n}b_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{b}_{i}=b_{i}-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{m=1}^{n}b_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> per expert <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly for <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cost matrix with respect to gating weights</span> is defined as <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\{C_{i,j}\}_{1\leq i\leq n,1\leq j\leq n}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{C_{i,j}\}_{1\leq i\leq n,1\leq j\leq n}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S5.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i,j}=\left(\|\widehat{W}_{i}-\widehat{W}^{\prime}_{j}\|_{2}^{2}+(\hat{b}_{i}-\hat{b}^{\prime}_{j})^{2}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i,j}=\left(\|\widehat{W}_{i}-\widehat{W}^{\prime}_{j}\|_{2}^{2}+(\hat{b}_{i}-\hat{b}^{\prime}_{j})^{2}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">measuring dissimilarity between centered gating parameters of expert <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and expert <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Method 2 - Internal Weights.</span> Experts exhibit permutation invariance in hidden neuron orderings. We use Gram matrices for a permutation-invariant representation (see Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Proof of the Permutation-Invariant Property for Equation () ‣ Appendix D Technical Details for Sections and ‣ On Linear Mode Connectivity of Mixture-of-Experts Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>). The <span id="S5.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cost matrix with respect to expert internal weights</span> is defined as <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\{C_{i,j}\}_{1\leq i\leq n,1\leq j\leq n}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{C_{i,j}\}_{1\leq i\leq n,1\leq j\leq n}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S5.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i,j}=\left(\left\|(\widetilde{A}_{i})^{\top}\widetilde{A}_{i}-(\widetilde{A}^{\prime}_{j})^{\top}\widetilde{A}^{\prime}_{j}\right\|_{F}^{2}+\left\|\widetilde{B}_{i}(\widetilde{B}_{i})^{\top}-\widetilde{B}^{\prime}_{j}(\widetilde{B}^{\prime}_{j})^{\top}\right\|_{F}^{2}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i,j}=\left(\left\|(\widetilde{A}_{i})^{\top}\widetilde{A}_{i}-(\widetilde{A}^{\prime}_{j})^{\top}\widetilde{A}^{\prime}_{j}\right\|_{F}^{2}+\left\|\widetilde{B}_{i}(\widetilde{B}_{i})^{\top}-\widetilde{B}^{\prime}_{j}(\widetilde{B}^{\prime}_{j})^{\top}\right\|_{F}^{2}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">quantifying dissimilarity between weight representations of expert <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and expert <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For both methods, the optimal expert ordering <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by solving the LAP: <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\arg\min_{\pi\in\text{S}_{n}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}C_{i,\pi(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mtext>S</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\arg\min_{\pi\in\text{S}_{n}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}C_{i,\pi(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aligning Expert Internal Weights.</span> After finding the permutation <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> to align experts between <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we align the internal weights of each matched expert pair <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,\tau(i))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,\tau(i))</annotation></semantics></math> using established MLP alignment methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>)</cite>, specifically the Weight Matching algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>)</cite>. This LAP-based algorithm aligns two MLPs with one hidden layer of size <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(h^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(h^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> time, ensuring efficient scaling. Applying this to each pair <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,\tau(i))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,\tau(i))</annotation></semantics></math> guarantees consistent neuron ordering across aligned experts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Weight Matching Algorithm</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a Weight Matching Algorithm designed to align MoEs by addressing permutation invariance in expert ordering and internal parameters, as detailed in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ D.3 Weight Matching Algorithm for Mixture-of-Experts ‣ Appendix D Technical Details for Sections and ‣ On Linear Mode Connectivity of Mixture-of-Experts Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The algorithm employs gate-based and expert-based methods to optimally permute experts, with both performing comparably well in loss barrier evaluations during model interpolation (see Section <a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3 Expert Order Matching Method ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ On Linear Mode Connectivity of Mixture-of-Experts Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>). Operating solely in parameter space, the algorithm avoids data-dependent computational overhead, with a complexity of <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{3}+nh^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{3}+nh^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring scalability for large-scale MoE alignment tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Experiments</h2>

<figure id="S6.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Comparison of the two expert matching methods for 12-layer ViT-MoE models with 4 experts on CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100, showing Rank (out of 24 permutations) and <math id="S6.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{L}</annotation></semantics></math> across layer placements, averaged over 10 checkpoint pairs. See Tables <a href="#A7.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ G.3 Expert Matching Method ‣ Appendix G Experimental Results ‣ On Linear Mode Connectivity of Mixture-of-Experts Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and <a href="#A7.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ G.3 Expert Matching Method ‣ Appendix G Experimental Results ‣ On Linear Mode Connectivity of Mixture-of-Experts Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> for SMoE and DeepSeekMoE.</figcaption>
<div id="S6.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:740.9pt;height:73.6pt;vertical-align:-34.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<p id="S6.T2.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">
