</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper presents a systematic analysis of LMC in MoE models. We show that functional equivalence via permutation symmetries is sufficient to construct low-loss linear paths–when they exist–between independently trained models. To enable this, we introduce an algorithm for identifying such permutations. Empirical evaluations confirm the presence of LMC across a wide range of MoE configurations–including dense, sparse, and shared-expert variants–under diverse hyperparameter settings and across datasets of varying scales and modalities. While our method does not provide theoretical bounds on the loss barrier, this remains a common limitation across prior LMC studies. Overall, our work offers a principled foundation for extending LMC analysis to other architectures, such as Transformers and State Space Models.</p>
</div>
</section>
